#
# Name every pstop USB-NCM tether esp-pstop<N>, N = the lowest index not already
# in use, so several units on one host get distinct, predictable names --
# esp-pstop0, esp-pstop1, ... -- with no per-unit registration. The pstop-port
# profile (match esp-pstop*) picks each one up and puts it on the pstop-br bridge.
#
# N is allocated by the esp-pstop-name helper (installed by install.sh), NOT by
# the kernel's usb<N> index (%n): the kernel reuses the lowest free usb<N>, so
# after the first unit is renamed away from usb0 the second unit ALSO comes up
# as usb0 and a NAME="esp-pstop%n" rename collides (EEXIST) -- the unit keeps
# the name usb0, matches no profile and gets no DHCP. Same symptom as the even
# earlier 70-esp-pstop.link, which pinned one fixed name to the VID:PID.
#
# Matches the app-mode device by VID:PID (303a:4001) on the USB parent, so it
# ignores download mode (303a:1001) and needs no ID_* imports. Sorted before
# 80-net-setup-link.rules, whose .link handling (including Ubuntu's
# 73-usb-net-by-mac.link -> enx<mac>) only applies while NAME is still empty.
# Install: install.sh (rule + helper) ; or by hand:
#   sudo cp 79-esp-pstop.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo install -D -m 0755 esp-pstop-name /usr/local/lib/udev/esp-pstop-name
#   sudo udevadm control --reload
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ATTRS{idVendor}=="303a", ATTRS{idProduct}=="4001", PROGRAM="/usr/local/lib/udev/esp-pstop-name", NAME="%c"
